WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.112
The first stage creates normalized versions of the messy customer fields.

00:00:06.112 --> 00:00:13.687
For the name, we trim surrounding spaces, collapse repeated internal spaces, and convert an empty result to null.

00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:21.062
For the email, we trim the text, convert it to lowercase, and convert a blank to null.

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.472
For the phone, we keep only digits.

00:00:24.472 --> 00:00:27.864
If no digits remain, the result becomes null.

00:00:28.314 --> 00:00:30.096
The signup date is different.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:37.606
At this stage, we preserve the original date text exactly as it came from the source.

00:00:37.606 --> 00:00:40.901
Why? Because date parsing is a separate decision.

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:47.661
Keeping normalization and parsing separate makes the pipeline easier to understand and debug.

00:00:47.661 --> 00:00:51.016
Each stage should do one clear job.

00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:56.328
That way, if a value looks wrong later, we can trace where the change occurred.
